# Worktree procedure — <project>

## Topology

<!-- The raw facts from scripts/detect-topology.sh, plus the human summary (single
     repo / split-git services + unversioned wrapper / classic multi-repo / …).
     Do not assume this generalizes to other projects — topology is detected
     per-project, never inherited as a default. -->

## Participants & scope

<!-- Which directories/repos participate in worktree work at all (some may never
     need one — e.g. a docs-only sibling repo). The branch-naming convention used
     when work spans more than one participant, so they stay coordinated. Only
     touched participants get worktreed — never "all or nothing." -->

## Tier decision

<!-- The concrete, project-specific signal that distinguishes:
     - Tier 0 (not parallel to anything — no worktree)
     - Tier 1 (code-only parallelism — no services/DB touched)
     - Tier 2 (full environment isolation — runs migrations / needs the stack
       live / mutates shared state)
     Record the concrete signal (e.g. "touches migrations/" or "runs
     docker compose up"), not a vague description. -->

## Tier 2 infra strategy

<!-- Only relevant if Tier 2 is reachable at all. Which strategy applies:
     clone full infra, clone partial infra (same DB server, new DB name), share
     with logical partitioning, or not yet available. If not yet available, note
     exactly what's missing (this doc records the recipe; it does not author new
     worktree-up scripts for the project). -->

## Lifecycle & cleanup

<!-- Ephemeral (spin up -> run -> auto-teardown) or persistent (iterate for
     hours/days -> explicit teardown on exit). What exactly must be cleaned up
     when a Tier-2 worktree closes: isolated DB dropped, isolated compose
     project/containers/network stopped (never shared named volumes), allocated
     port offset freed, worktree directory kept or removed. If N worktrees can
     run at once, the concrete, deterministic port/offset allocation rule (not
     just "offset by index" — how collisions between concurrent sessions are
     avoided). -->

